{
  "dom_id": "step-q90529",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-4,\\, & 2\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we compute $\\text{E}[X]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}x\\cdot (2x-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}(2{x}^{2}-4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-2{x}^{2})|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot (2x-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}(2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3})|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{6}-{(\\frac{8}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
